diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..f4d11e6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,371 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Bots are members of rooms that post messages over HTTP, and that are told over HTTP (webhooks) about mentions of them and about the rooms they join.
+A bot is set up by a chat member and then driven by a shell script or CI job, which authenticates with the bot's
+code.
+
+## Setting up a bot (people)
+
+1. Open account settings (`/account/edit`) and click the bot icon, "Set up chat bots" (`/bots`).
+2. "Add a chat bot" opens `/bots/new`, unless the member is at the limit (see "Limiting bots per member"). The bot
+   name is required. The avatar, the webhook URL and the "Add to all open rooms" switch are optional. The switch is
+   off by default (see below). "Save changes" creates the bot and returns to `/bots`. (Webhooks are described below.)
+3. Each bot on the list shows its name and its **bot code** in a read-only "Bot code" field. Copy it into your
+   script or CI secrets. Treat it like a password.
+4. "Edit <name>" changes the name and avatar. "Delete this chat bot" (with a confirmation) removes the bot.
+
+Who sees and manages what:
+
+- Any chat member can create bots, up to the account's bots per member limit (see "Limiting bots per member"). A
+  member sees, edits and deletes only the bots they created.
+- Administrators see and manage every bot, and they alone can reactivate a suspended one (see "Stopping misbehaving
+  bots").
+- The bots page lists all of them on one page (no pagination).
+- Bots are left out of the people list on the account page, since they're managed from `/bots`.
+
+Bots do not join open rooms by default, unlike people. The "Add to all open rooms" switch makes a bot a member of every
+open room: the rooms that exist when the switch is turned on, and each open room created later. Turning the switch off
+keeps the bot in the rooms it is already in, and only stops new open rooms from adding it. Bots can be added to closed
+rooms the same way people are (the access switches in the room's settings).
+
+A bot that is not in a room can't post there: the message endpoint answers `404 Not Found` (see "Posting messages").
+
+## Limiting bots per member
+
+Administrators set **Bots per member** on the account settings page (`/account/edit`, shown to administrators only).
+It's a whole number, 5 by default. Zero is allowed; blank and negative values are refused.
+
+- A member can have at most that many bots. A bot counts until it's deleted, so deleting one frees its place.
+- Administrators aren't limited.
+- At the limit, `/bots` doesn't offer "Add a chat bot" and says "You’ve reached the limit of 5 chat bots". Opening
+  `/bots/new` and posting to `/bots` both answer `403 Forbidden`. The bot model refuses the creation as well, so no
+  other path can create a bot past the limit.
+- Lowering the limit never removes bots. A member keeps every bot they have, can still edit and delete them, and can't
+  add more until they're under the new limit. Raising the limit lets them add more.
+
+## Posting messages (bots)
+
+The raw request body is the message. There is no JSON envelope and there are no field names:
+
+```sh
+curl -d "Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✅" \
+  https://chat.example.com/rooms/<room-id>/<bot-code>/messages
+```
+
+| Response | When |
+| --- | --- |
+| `201 Created`, `Location: /messages/:message_id` | The message was posted. |
+| `422 Unprocessable Content` | The body is empty or only whitespace, or it isn't valid UTF-8. |
+| `404 Not Found` | The room doesn't exist, or the bot isn't a member of it. |
+| `401 Unauthorized` (with `WWW-Authenticate: Bot realm="Campfire"`) | The bot code is unknown, the bot was deleted, or its user was deactivated or banned. |
+| `403 Forbidden` | The bot is suspended (see "Stopping misbehaving bots"). |
+| `429 Too Many Requests` (with `Retry-After`, the seconds left) | The bot is posting too fast, or it's still cooling down. Nothing is posted. |
+
+Details:
+
+- The body is text, whatever `Content-Type` the client sends. `curl -d` sends form encoding by default, and
+  that doesn't change the message: `Disk at 100% done` is posted as written.
+- Line breaks are kept. Markup is shown as text, so `<b>` in a message appears literally.
+- Attachments aren't supported. The body is the whole message.
+- The message shows the bot's name as its author. It's broadcast to the room, updates unread markers, and
+  triggers push notifications exactly like a person's message.
+- `Location` is `/messages/:id`, as specified. Messages are room-scoped in this app, so that URL returns 404
+  today. A future change could resolve it to the room's message.
+
+## Receiving mentions (webhooks)
+
+A bot can have a **webhook URL**, set in the "Webhook URL" field on `/bots/new` or the bot's edit page. When a
+person mentions the bot (`@Deploy Bot`) in a message, Campfire POSTs the mention to that URL. The bot answers either
+in the HTTP response, or later by posting through its bot code. Leaving the field empty removes the webhook.
+
+The request is `POST <webhook URL>` with `Content-Type: application/json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event":  "mention",
+  "author": { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+  "room":   { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot-code>/messages" },
+  "message": {
+    "id": 118,
+    "body": {
+      "text": "restart the build please",
+      "html": "<div>… full rich-text body …</div>"
+    },
+    "link": "/rooms/5/@118"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+- `event` is `mention` for a mention. A bot is also told when it joins a room, with the `joined` event (see "Joining rooms").
+- `message.body.text` is the message as text, with the bot's own @mention taken out, so it reads as the command
+  addressed to the bot. Other mentions read as `@Name`.
+- `message.body.html` is the whole rich-text body, cleaned the way the room cleans messages. Mentions are
+  `action-text-attachment` elements, and their `sgid` identifies the person mentioned.
+- `room.reply_to` is the bot's own posting URL for that room. POST text to it to reply later (see "Posting messages").
+- `message.link` opens the room scrolled to the message.
+- Paths are relative. The bot knows its Campfire origin, so `https://chat.example.com` plus `reply_to` is the URL.
+- In a direct message room, `room.name` is the other people in it.
+
+The bot replies with:
+
+| Response | Result |
+| --- | --- |
+| `200` with `Content-Type: text/plain` | The body is posted into the room as the bot's message. Line breaks are kept and markup is shown as text. |
+| `200` with `Content-Type: text/html` | The body is posted as the bot's rich text. Scripts, images and other tags the room doesn't display are removed, and so are mentions and other attachments. |
+| `204` | Nothing is posted. |
+| Anything else | Nothing is posted. Redirects aren't followed. |
+
+A `200` body that is blank posts nothing. A body over 64 KB posts nothing (it isn't truncated). A charset in
+`Content-Type` is honoured, and a body that isn't valid text in its charset posts nothing.
+
+Timing and failures:
+
+- Webhooks are called from a background job, so posting a message never waits on a bot. An answer appears a
+  moment after the mention.
+- The bot has 5 seconds to connect, 15 seconds between reads, and 30 seconds for the whole exchange.
+- A webhook that fails, times out or is refused posts nothing. Failures are logged without the URL or the payload.
+  There are no retries. Each failure counts towards suspending the bot, and a 2xx resets that count (see "Stopping
+  misbehaving bots").
+- A reply is a message like any other, so it counts towards the bot's posting limit. A reply refused by the limit is
+  not posted.
+
+What asks a webhook:
+
+- A mention of the bot in a message that a person posts, while the bot is a member of that room. A closed room needs
+  access granted first, as for people.
+- A bot's own messages never ask a bot, so two bots can't keep each other talking. Bot answers can't mention anyone
+  anyway (see below).
+- Only when the message is posted. Editing a message to add a mention doesn't ask, the same as push notifications.
+- Never when the bot has no webhook URL, and never when the bot is suspended (see "Stopping misbehaving bots").
+
+Webhook URLs must be `http` or `https`. Campfire fetches webhooks the same way it fetches links for unfurling, so
+addresses that are private or internal (loopback, private networks, link-local) are refused. A webhook on the same
+private network as Campfire doesn't work today. See "Known limits (webhooks)".
+
+## Joining rooms (webhooks)
+
+Whenever a bot is added to a room, its webhook receives a `joined` event, once for each room it joins. That holds
+whether a person added the bot to a closed room, a person turned its switch on (which adds it to the open rooms), or
+a new open room picked it up when it was created. Direct conversations never send it, and neither does a bot without a webhook URL or a suspended bot.
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot-code>/messages" }
+}
+```
+
+- `room.id` and `room.name` are the room. `room.reply_to` is the bot's posting URL for the room, as for a mention.
+- The bot answers the same way as for a mention. A `200` with `Content-Type: text/plain` or `text/html` is posted into
+  the room as the bot's message, so a bot can say hello that way. A `204`, or any other response, posts nothing.
+- The event is sent once the membership has committed. A membership that is rolled back sends nothing, and a bot that
+  has left the room by the time its webhook answers doesn't post.
+- Adding a bot back after it was removed is a new join, so it is announced again. Granting a bot a room it is already
+  in announces nothing.
+
+## Stopping misbehaving bots
+
+A bot that floods a room, or whose webhook keeps failing, is suspended automatically. The limits below are fixed in
+code. They have no settings.
+
+**Flooding.** A bot may post at most 10 messages in any 10 seconds. Messages posted through the message endpoint and
+webhook replies both count, across all of the bot's rooms. A message counts while it's less than 10 seconds old.
+
+- The message that goes over the limit is refused. The endpoint answers `429 Too Many Requests` with `Retry-After` set
+  to the whole seconds left, and a webhook reply isn't posted. The refusal starts a 30-second cooldown.
+- During the cooldown, every attempt to post is refused the same way, with the seconds left. Each such attempt counts,
+  and the fifth attempt during one cooldown suspends the bot. That fifth attempt still gets `429`. From then on the bot
+  gets `403`.
+- Attempts during a cooldown don't extend it. Each cooldown counts its attempts from zero.
+
+**Failing webhooks.** A delivery fails when the webhook answers with anything other than `2xx`, when it times out, or
+when it can't be connected to. That includes an address refused for being private. Mentions and joined events are both
+deliveries. Ten failed deliveries in a row suspend the bot, so a bot whose webhook fails from the start is suspended
+within ten of its first events. A delivery that gets a `2xx` resets the count, whether or not the answer is posted.
+
+**Suspended bots.**
+
+- The posting endpoint answers `403 Forbidden`, for any room and any body.
+- The bot gets no webhooks: no mentions and no joined events. A job queued before the suspension does nothing when it
+  runs, and isn't counted as a failed delivery.
+- Its rooms, memberships and messages stay as they are, and it still counts towards its owner's bot limit.
+- The bots page keeps it listed, with **Suspended** and the reason, for example "Suspended: Its webhook failed 10 times
+  in a row." Its creator sees this too, but can't reactivate it.
+- Only administrators can reactivate it. On `/bots`, an administrator clicks **Reactivate** and confirms. Reactivation
+  clears the suspension, the failure count and any cooldown. Nothing reactivates a bot on its own, and editing a bot
+  doesn't reactivate it.
+
+## Design notes
+
+**Data model.** A bot is a `bots` row (`user_id`, `creator_id`, `bot_key`) that points at an ordinary `users`
+row. The user holds the bot's name, avatar and room memberships, so everything that handles people (membership,
+mentions, messages, push, avatars) works for bots without changes. The credential lives only in `bots`, so it
+never appears when users are serialized or listed.
+
+**Bot limit.** `bots_per_member` is a `has_json` setting in `accounts.settings`, next to `restrict_room_creation_to_administrators`.
+`User#can_create_bot?` counts the member's `Bot.created_by` rows, which are the rows the bots page lists, so a bot counts
+until it's deleted (a banned bot is still listed, so it still counts). The limit is a validation on `Bot` with `on: :create`,
+so updates never hit it and lowering the limit only blocks new bots. The validation runs inside the save transaction, and
+the app's SQLite transactions start in `immediate` mode, so two creates by one member are serialized and can't both pass the
+count. The controller's `403` gives a clear response, but the model check is the one nothing can bypass. The setting's
+validation uses string messages: a symbol type makes Rails read the attribute from `Account`, which has no such method.
+
+**Bot users can't sign in.** The `users.password_digest` column is `NOT NULL`, so a bot's user gets a random
+password that nobody learns. A bot has no email address, and its profile doesn't offer the auto-login link.
+
+**Deleting a bot** deactivates its user, the same way removing a person does: the user leaves every room (direct
+rooms excepted), and its messages stay. The `bots` row is destroyed, which kills the bot code. Deactivated and
+banned users fail authentication too (`Bot.active`).
+
+**Posting.** `Bots::MessagesController` is unauthenticated by session and doesn't check CSRF tokens, because
+the bot code in the path is the credential. Rails parses form bodies before any action runs, and it rejects
+text such as `100% done` with a 400. The controller therefore declares the body as not being form fields
+(`process_action`). `Bots::MessagesControllerTest` covers this.
+
+**Webhooks.** `Room#receive` enqueues `Room::AskMentionedBotsJob` for each new message, the same way it enqueues push
+notifications. That job finds the mentioned bots (`Message#mentioned_bots`) and enqueues one `Bot::WebhookJob` per
+bot, so one slow webhook doesn't hold up the others. `Bot::Webhook` builds the payload, makes the request and posts
+the answer. Nothing on the posting request's path talks to a webhook, and the job arguments are GlobalIDs, so the
+queue never holds a payload or a bot code.
+
+**Joins.** Bots' memberships are granted through `room.memberships.grant_to`, which every path that adds a bot uses:
+creating a room, granting access to a closed room, opening a room, and the switch. It asks the database which
+memberships it inserted (`INSERT ... RETURNING`), so a bot is announced only when its membership is new, and a bot that
+is already in the room isn't counted twice. `Room#announce_joined_bots` then queues a `Bot::WebhookJob` for each such
+bot that has a webhook, through `Room.current_transaction.after_commit`: outside a transaction that runs at once, and
+inside one it waits for the commit. A rolled-back grant queues nothing, and a job never runs before the membership it
+announces exists.
+
+**Who joins open rooms.** People are granted every open room when they are created (`User#grant_membership_to_open_rooms`,
+which skips bots). A bot gets open rooms only through its switch, a boolean column on `bots` (`join_all_open_rooms`,
+default off). `Bot#join_open_rooms` runs in the save that turns the switch on, and `Rooms::Open` grants a new open room
+to `User.joining_open_rooms`: people, plus the bots whose switch is on.
+
+**Fetching webhooks** uses `RestrictedHTTP::PrivateNetworkGuard`, as link unfurling does (see `SECURITY.md`: any member
+can make Campfire fetch a URL, so the destination is checked). The checked address is pinned with `ipaddr:`, so a DNS
+change between the check and the connection can't move the request to an internal host. Redirects aren't followed,
+the body is read only up to 64 KB, and a `Timeout` caps the whole exchange, so a bot can't keep a worker busy by
+dripping bytes slowly.
+
+**Answers from bots** go through the same sanitizing filters the room uses on display
+(`ContentFilters::SanitizeTags` and `SanitizeAttributes`) before they're stored, and all `action-text-attachment`
+elements are removed first. An attachment in a bot's answer would notify the person it names as if they had written
+it, so bots can't create mentions.
+
+**The bot code in the payload.** `room.reply_to` contains the bot code, so the webhook's recipient learns it. That's
+intended: the webhook is the bot. Apart from the bots page, that's the only place the code leaves Campfire, and the payload is never logged.
+
+**Webhook URLs are secrets** (they often carry a token in the path or query). The parameter filter masks
+`webhook_url` in request logs and in `inspect`, and the bot's edit page isn't cached.
+
+**Authorization.** Editing and deleting use `Current.user.can_administer?(bot)`, which means administrator or
+creator, the same rule used for other records here. Reactivating is administrators only (`Bots::ReactivationsController`,
+`POST /bots/:id/reactivation`).
+
+**Suspension and flood control.** The state lives on the `bots` row: `suspended_at` and `suspension_reason`
+(`flooding` or `failing_webhook`), `consecutive_webhook_failures`, and `cooldown_until` with `cooldown_attempts`.
+`Bot::Suspendable` holds the suspension, the reasons and the failure count. `Bot::Throttle` holds the flood rules.
+`Bot#post_html` is the only way a bot's message is created, and it always goes through the throttle, so the API and
+webhook replies are limited the same way.
+
+- The window is counted from the bot's own `messages` rows, not from a log of posts kept alongside them. A message is
+  already the record of a post, so the two can't disagree. The cost: an administrator who deletes a message removes it
+  from the count.
+- The check, the cooldown bookkeeping and the insert run inside `bot.with_lock`. The app's SQLite transactions start in
+  immediate mode, so the lock serializes a bot's concurrent posts, and two requests can't both pass the count.
+- A refusal is raised only after the transaction commits. The cooldown, the attempt count and a suspension are saved
+  even though the message isn't, so a refused attempt is still counted. Raising inside the transaction would roll them
+  back.
+- The posting endpoint checks suspension before it looks up the room, so a suspended bot gets `403` in any room. The
+  throttle checks it again under the lock, for a suspension that happens during the request.
+- Webhook outcomes are recorded in `Bot::Webhook#ask_webhook`: a 2xx after the response is read, or a failure in the
+  rescue. Both go through `with_lock`, so the count is read and written against the latest state.
+- Suspended bots are left out before a job is queued (`Bot.receiving_webhooks`, used for mentions and joined events).
+  `Bot::Webhook#deliverable?` checks again when the job runs, because the state can change while it waits.
+- Refusals are `Bot::Suspended` (403) and `Bot::CoolingDown`, which carries `retry_after` for the 429 and its
+  `Retry-After` header. A webhook reply that's refused is logged at info level, without its text.
+- The limits are constants: `Bot::Throttle` (`MESSAGES`, `WINDOW`, `COOLDOWN`, `ATTEMPTS`) and
+  `Bot::Suspendable::FAILED_DELIVERY_LIMIT`. Nothing in settings changes them.
+
+## Protecting bot codes
+
+A bot code grants the ability to post as the bot, so:
+
+- It's shown only on `/bots`, to the bot's creator and administrators. The edit page doesn't show it.
+- `/bots` is served with `Cache-Control: no-store` and `turbo-cache-control: no-cache`, so neither the browser nor
+  Turbo's page cache keeps a copy.
+- It never appears in messages, broadcasts, redirects, flash messages or JSON. The one exception is `room.reply_to`
+  in a webhook payload, which goes only to that bot's own webhook (see "Receiving mentions").
+- Rails' parameter filter masks it (`bot_key` matches the existing `_key` filter), so `Parameters:` log lines show
+  `[FILTERED]`.
+- Rails masks only the query string when it logs a path, so `lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_path.rb` masks the code in
+  the path too: `Started POST "/rooms/1/[FILTERED]/messages"`. `Bot.filter_path` does the same for Sentry request
+  URLs (`config/initializers/sentry.rb`).
+- The code is generated by `has_secure_token` (24 base58 characters, about 140 bits) and is unique in the database.
+
+Known limits:
+
+- The code is stored in plain text, because the page has to show it again. Anyone with database access can read it.
+- ActiveRecord's SQL debug logging prints the value in the `bots.bot_key` query, in development or with
+  `RAILS_LOG_LEVEL=debug`. Session tokens already appear the same way. Production logs at `info` by default.
+- Reverse-proxy access logs record the full path, which includes the code. Treat those logs as sensitive.
+
+Not built yet: rotating a bot code (`regenerate_bot_key` is available), rate limiting failed code attempts,
+per-bot scopes, and a size limit on message bodies (any size is accepted today).
+
+Known limits (posting limits and suspension):
+
+- The 10-in-10 window counts the bot's messages in the database, so deleting one of them removes it from the count. Only
+  administrators can delete a bot's messages.
+- The limits apply per bot, across its rooms. There's no per-room limit.
+- Nobody is told when a bot is suspended. Its creator sees it on `/bots` and an administrator has to reactivate it.
+- A suspended bot's joined events aren't queued, so reactivating it doesn't replay the joins it missed.
+- The flood rules and the failure count are in code. Changing them means a deploy.
+
+Known limits (webhooks):
+
+- Private and internal webhook addresses are refused, so a bot whose service runs on the same private network as
+  Campfire can't use a webhook yet. Allowing them would be a deliberate, per-deployment decision.
+- Requests aren't signed, so the webhook URL is the only secret. A receiver can't tell a genuine mention from a
+  forged POST unless the URL is secret.
+- There are no retries and no delivery log. A failed webhook is only logged, by class.
+- Sentry's HTTP breadcrumbs record a webhook's scheme, host and path (not its query) when an error is reported after a
+  webhook call. They go to the operator's own `SENTRY_DSN`.
+- Replies over 64 KB are dropped, not truncated.
+- Edits that add a mention don't ask the bot.
+- Turning the switch on sends one `joined` event per open room, each a separate webhook call. There is no batching.
+
+## Code map
+
+- `app/models/account.rb` (`bots_per_member` setting, validated as a count)
+- `app/models/bot.rb` (credential, creator, name/avatar delegated to the user, webhook URL, `post_text`/`post_html`, `deactivate`, the limit check on create)
+- `app/models/user.rb` (`has_one :bot`, `bot?`, `without_bots`, `joining_open_rooms`, `can_create_bot?`)
+- `app/models/rooms/open.rb` (grants a new open room to the people and the bots that join all open rooms)
+- `app/controllers/bots_controller.rb` (`/bots` management pages; `new` and `create` are refused at the limit)
+- `app/controllers/bots/messages_controller.rb` (the posting API)
+- `app/views/bots/` (list, form, new, edit; the list shows the limit message in place of "Add a chat bot")
+- `app/views/accounts/edit.html.erb` (the "Bots per member" field, for administrators)
+- `lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_path.rb` (log masking)
+- `db/migrate/*_create_bots.rb`, `db/migrate/*_add_webhook_url_to_bots.rb`, `db/migrate/*_add_join_all_open_rooms_to_bots.rb`
+- `app/models/bot/webhook.rb` (the webhook call for a mention or a join: payload, request, reply posting)
+- `app/jobs/bot/webhook_job.rb` (one webhook call, per bot and mention or join)
+- `app/jobs/room/ask_mentioned_bots_job.rb` (finds the mentioned bots for a message, one webhook job each)
+- `app/models/message/mentionee.rb` (`mentioned_bots`, the bots a message asks)
+- `app/models/room.rb` (`receive` enqueues the ask job; `memberships.grant_to` and `announce_joined_bots` send joined events)
+- `app/models/bot/suspendable.rb` (suspension, reasons, reactivation, the failed-delivery count)
+- `app/models/bot/throttle.rb` (the flood limit, the cooldown and its attempts)
+- `app/controllers/bots/reactivations_controller.rb` (administrators reactivate a suspended bot)
+- `app/views/bots/_bot.html.erb` (the "Suspended" line with its reason, and the administrators' "Reactivate" button)
+- `db/migrate/*_add_suspension_and_cooldown_to_bots.rb`
+
+Tests: `test/models/account_test.rb` (the setting), `test/models/user_test.rb` (`can_create_bot?`),
+`test/models/bot_test.rb`, `test/models/bot/webhook_test.rb`, `test/models/bot/throttle_test.rb` (the flood limit and
+cooldown), `test/models/bot/suspendable_test.rb` (suspension and the failure count), `test/models/message_test.rb` (mention cases),
+`test/models/room_test.rb` (joined events), `test/models/rooms/open_test.rb` (bots and new open rooms),
+`test/jobs/bot/webhook_job_test.rb`, `test/jobs/room/ask_mentioned_bots_job_test.rb`,
+`test/controllers/bots_controller_test.rb` (the limit on the list, `new` and `create`),
+`test/controllers/accounts_controller_test.rb` (the field), `test/controllers/bots/messages_controller_test.rb` (429, 403 and
+Retry-After), `test/controllers/bots/reactivations_controller_test.rb`, `test/controllers/bots_controller_test.rb` (the
+Suspended line and Reactivate button),
+`test/lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_path_test.rb`, plus bot cases in the accounts, users, autocomplete, messages and
+closed-room controller tests.
diff --git a/lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_path.rb b/lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_path.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..18ee353
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_path.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,10 @@
+# Rails masks only the query string when it logs a request path. The bot message endpoint carries its bot key in the
+# path, so the path is masked here too. That covers "Started POST" log lines and instrumentation. Error reports take
+# their URL from the request, so config/initializers/sentry.rb masks it there.
+module FilteredBotPath
+  def filtered_path
+    Bot.filter_path(super)
+  end
+end
+
+ActionDispatch::Request.prepend FilteredBotPath
